#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\uparrow\\rangle\\sim|0\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\uparrow\\rangle\\sim|0\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: dropped (table row count mismatch: source 0, translation 1): $$ |\uparrow\rangle\sim|0\rangle. $$
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Code quantum circuits to create the desired initial state, and then use either real hardware or a simulator to check the outcomes of a single measurement, and from an ensemble of hundreds or even thousands of measurements.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fifth experiment: Measuring different observables {#fifth-experiment-measuring-different-observables}"
msgstr "Quinto experimento: Medindo diferentes observáveis {#fifth-experiment-measuring-different-observables}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"So far we have only made measurements along the z axis. In a Stern-Gerlach experiment, if we wanted to measure along, say, the x-axis we would simply orient the inhomogeneous magnetic field to point along the $x$ axis, and look for deflections along $x$ on the screen. IBM quantum computers, however, are designed to make measurements along only one axis ($z$). To measure a state along $x$ we must perform a \"change of basis\". This means we must perform an operation that takes states along $x$ in the Bloch sphere to $z$, and vice-versa. There are a few ways of implementing this, but the preferred way is a Hadamard gate:\n"
"$$\n"
"H=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Até agora, fizemos medições apenas ao longo do eixo z. Em um experimento de Stern-Gerlach, se quiséssemos medir ao longo do eixo x, por exemplo, bastaria orientar o campo magnético não-homogêneo na direção do eixo $x$ e observar as deflexões ao longo de $x$ na tela. Os computadores quânticos da IBM, porém, são projetados para realizar medições em apenas um eixo ($z$). Para medir um estado ao longo de $x$, é necessário realizar uma \"mudança de base\". Isso significa que precisamos realizar uma operação que leve os estados ao longo de $x$ na esfera de Bloch para $z$, e vice-versa. Existem algumas formas de implementar isso, mas a abordagem preferida é a porta Hadamard:\n"
"$$\n"
"H=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-4}"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Show that $H|0\\rangle = |+\\rangle_x$ and that $H|+\\rangle_x = |0\\rangle$\n"
msgstr "Mostre que $H|0\\rangle = |+\\rangle_x$ e que $H|+\\rangle_x = |0\\rangle$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H|0\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = |+\\rangle_x\n"
"$$\n"
"$$\n"
"H|+\\rangle_x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt(2)}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = |0\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"H|0\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = |+\\rangle_x\n"
"$$\n"
"$$\n"
"H|+\\rangle_x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt(2)}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = |0\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Show that $H|1\\rangle = |-\\rangle_x$ and that $H|-\\rangle_x = |1\\rangle$\n"
msgstr "Mostre que $H|1\\rangle = |-\\rangle_x$ e que $H|-\\rangle_x = |1\\rangle$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H|1\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} = |-\\rangle_x\n"
"$$\n"
"$$\n"
"H|-\\rangle_x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt(2)}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = |1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"H|1\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} = |-\\rangle_x\n"
"$$\n"
"$$\n"
"H|-\\rangle_x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt(2)}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = |1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/fa1ebdb9-ea6b-4f38-bb8e-489ea989fc6b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/fa1ebdb9-ea6b-4f38-bb8e-489ea989fc6b-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This circuit rotates our system so that measurements (along $z$) tell us about the $x$ characteristics prior to the rotation. We already know that the quantum computer initializes states to $|0\\rangle$ which corresponds to $|\\uparrow\\rangle$. Let's see what happens when we measure the projection of spin along $x$ for a state initially in $|\\uparrow\\rangle$. We show this step on a simulator to encourage you to explore the statistics of other circuits with different initializations.\n"
msgstr "Este circuito rotaciona nosso sistema de forma que as medições (ao longo de $z$) nos forneçam informações sobre as características em $x$ anteriores à rotação. Já sabemos que o computador quântico inicializa os estados em $|0\\rangle$, o que corresponde a $|\\uparrow\\rangle$. Vamos ver o que acontece quando medimos a projeção do spin ao longo de $x$ para um estado inicialmente em $|\\uparrow\\rangle$. Mostramos esse passo em um simulador para encorajar você a explorar as estatísticas de outros circuitos com diferentes inicializações.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This shows us that for a qubit initially in $|0\\rangle$ (or equivalently, a spin in the $|\\uparrow\\rangle$) the probability of measuring a projection along $+x$ or $-x$ is about 50-50. In some ways, this makes perfect sense. After all, if something were pointing along the z direction, it would have no particular preference for $\\pm x$. We might expect the same to be true, if we start with the state $|1\\rangle$ (or $|\\downarrow\\rangle$). Let's check:\n"
msgstr "Isso nos mostra que, para um qubit inicialmente em $|0\\rangle$ (ou equivalentemente, um spin em $|\\uparrow\\rangle$), a probabilidade de medir uma projeção ao longo de $+x$ ou $-x$ é aproximadamente 50-50. De certa forma, isso faz todo sentido. Afinal, se algo estivesse apontando na direção z, não teria nenhuma preferência particular por $\\pm x$. Poderíamos esperar o mesmo se começarmos com o estado $|1\\rangle$ (ou $|\\downarrow\\rangle$). Vamos verificar:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/eb4f66b7-4f8a-4dd1-bf60-dcf7f9453807-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/eb4f66b7-4f8a-4dd1-bf60-dcf7f9453807-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Yes! Again, we find a 50-50 probability of measuring a projections along $\\pm x$. In analogy with measurements along $z$, we might further suspect that a particle that collapses into a state definitely along $+x$ might have zero probability of subsequently being measured along $-x$. Let's check:\n"
msgstr "Sim! Novamente, encontramos uma probabilidade de 50-50 de medir projeções ao longo de $\\pm x$. Por analogia com as medições ao longo de $z$, podemos suspeitar ainda que uma partícula que colapsa para um estado definitivamente ao longo de $+x$ tenha probabilidade zero de ser medida posteriormente ao longo de $-x$. Vamos verificar:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/41dbe93a-81c1-457b-887e-41253b6ac51b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/41dbe93a-81c1-457b-887e-41253b6ac51b-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/9561833d-4756-4e83-a226-352382c6b31b-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/stern-gerlach-measurements-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/9561833d-4756-4e83-a226-352382c6b31b-1.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As expected, we obtain results in which the projection is along $+x$, and then later is again along $+x$, and we have results in which the projection is initially along $-x$ and is later along $-x$ once again. We do not see cases where the projection switches from $+x$ to $-x$ or vice-versa. We can collect our observations and use them to develop further operators in matrix forms, and further state vectors.\n"
msgstr "Como esperado, obtemos resultados em que a projeção está ao longo de $+x$ e, posteriormente, novamente ao longo de $+x$; e também resultados em que a projeção está inicialmente ao longo de $-x$ e, mais tarde, novamente ao longo de $-x$. Não observamos casos em que a projeção muda de $+x$ para $-x$ ou vice-versa. Podemos reunir nossas observações e utilizá-las para desenvolver mais operadores na forma matricial e mais vetores de estado.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We know:\n"
"- States with definite projections along $+z$ or $-z$ have a 50% chance of being found to have a projection along $+x$ and 50% chance of having a projection along $-x$.\n"
"- States with a definite projection along $+x$ have zero chance of later being found to have a projection along $-x$, and vice-versa.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sabemos que:\n"
"- Estados com projeções definidas ao longo de $+z$ ou $-z$ têm 50% de chance de serem encontrados com projeção ao longo de $+x$ e 50% de chance de terem projeção ao longo de $-x$.\n"
"- Estados com projeção definida ao longo de $+x$ têm probabilidade zero de serem encontrados posteriormente com projeção ao longo de $-x$, e vice-versa.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use these results to construct states with a definite, positive projection along $x$ (which we call $|+x\\rangle$), and those with definite, negative projection along $x$ (which we call $|-x\\rangle$). From those states, we can construct the matrix corresponding to $S_x$, exactly as we did for $S_z$. We leave these as exercises to the learner. Similarly, one can construct experiments making measurements along the $y$ axis, determine vectors for $|+y\\rangle$ and $|-y\\rangle$, and finally obtain an expression for $S_y$.\n"
msgstr "Podemos usar esses resultados para construir estados com projeção definida e positiva ao longo de $x$ (que chamamos de $|+x\\rangle$), e aqueles com projeção definida e negativa ao longo de $x$ (que chamamos de $|-x\\rangle$). A partir desses estados, podemos construir a matriz correspondente a $S_x$, exatamente como fizemos para $S_z$. Deixamos esses pontos como exercícios para o(a) estudante. Da mesma forma, é possível construir experimentos realizando medições ao longo do eixo $y$, determinar os vetores para $|+y\\rangle$ e $|-y\\rangle$, e por fim obter uma expressão para $S_y$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Collecting all these vectors and matrices together, we have\n"
msgstr "Reunindo todos esses vetores e matrizes, temos\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"|+x\\rangle &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  & |-x\\rangle &=& \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_x &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"|+y\\rangle &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ i\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  & |-y\\rangle &=& \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -i\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_y &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -i \\\\ i & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"|+z\\rangle &= \\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  &|-z\\rangle &=& \\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_z &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"|+x\\rangle &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  & |-x\\rangle &=& \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_x &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"|+y\\rangle &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ i\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  & |-y\\rangle &=& \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ -i\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_y &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -i \\\\ i & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"|+z\\rangle &= \\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} & \\:  &|-z\\rangle &=& \\begin{pmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} & \\: &S_z &=& \\frac{\\hbar}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

